I'm working from Chapter 10: Play 2 — The Kickoff Play. My situation: Our kickoff meetings are just a slide tour and a project timeline. Customers leave without knowing what success looks like. How should a kickoff actually work?
Materials: {{MATERIALS}}
Using these materials, draft a kickoff preparation brief. Include the customer's likely business objective, what was purchased and what was not, the stakeholders and their possible roles, open questions, potential loyalty blockers, likely First Value options, and the areas to confirm in the meeting. Keep known facts separate from assumptions. Then draft a pre-kickoff email that introduces the team, lists the attendees (including the seller), shares the agenda, and names anything the customer should prepare. Stay within this method. Ask me for any missing details instead of guessing.

I'm working from Chapter 10: Play 2 — The Kickoff Play. My situation: Our kickoff meetings are just a slide tour and a project timeline. Customers leave without knowing what success looks like. How should a kickoff actually work?
Materials: {{MATERIALS}}
Using these notes, draft a follow-through email that thanks the customer, summarizes the discussion, captures their answers to the Five Questions in their own words, lists decisions, gives action items with owners and due dates, states the agreed First Value goal, and confirms the next meeting. Then create a CRM update with fields for success definition, risk factors, executive sponsor, customer owners, First Value target, and next steps. Flag any Five Question that went unanswered or any action item without an owner. Stay within this method. Ask me for any missing details instead of guessing.
